          "type": "assistant",
          "text": "## TL;DR\n\nCross-lingual recall (discuss in Russian → search in English) **does not work in what's shipped, and that's by design**: phase 1 is lexical-only (`simple` FTS + `word_similarity` trigram), and both legs match surface forms — an English query shares zero tokens and zero trigrams with Cyrillic text. The designed answer is the **phase 2/3 multilingual embedding leg** (#196/#197), which is currently in the *deferred backlog*, not on the v0.7 critical path. The one path that can work *today* is the agent-facing one — exactly your scenario — via model-side query translation, but nothing currently makes that deterministic.\n\n## How each layer behaves in your scenario\n\n| Retrieval leg | RU chat, EN query | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| FTS (`simple`, no stemming, no lang detection) | ✗ | token-level surface match; different words, different script |\n| Trigram (`word_similarity`) | ✗ | helps typos/transliteration (\"Хабр\"↔\"habr\" partially), never translation |\n| Embeddings (phase 2/3, bge-m3-class multilingual, operator-config/Ollama) | ✓ designed | multilingual models place RU/ES/EN paraphrases near each other in vector space; ru/en/es are all first-tier languages for that model class (confidence: high) |\n\nWhat *does* work today even in a Russian chat: language-invariant tokens — code identifiers, error strings, package names, URLs, filenames, Latin-script product names. In practice a lot of technical recall queries are these.\n\nThe research corpus already anticipated your exact case: the cross-report locks in \"no language detection anywhere; `simple` FTS + trigrams + multilingual embeddings for ru/en/es,\" accepts the known cost (no Russian stemming hurts inflected lexical recall), and defers the fix to the embedding lift rather than multi-config tsvector unions — with the phase-1 eval set existing precisely so that lift is provable.\n\n## Three things worth noticing\n\n**1. Cross-lingual queries are effectively single-leg retrieval.** Once embeddings land, an EN query over a RU chat gets *only* a vector rank — lexical and trigram contribute nothing. RRF then has a subtle bias: an English chat with incidental term overlap collects lexical+vector contributions and can outrank the true Russian target that has vector-only evidence. Rank-based fusion softens this, but cross-lingual quality will be exactly as good as the embedding model, with no hybrid safety net. The eval set needs enough cross-language queries to see this, which leads to:\n\n**2. A real spec gap.** The chat-search spec's eval requirement lists `exact phrases, identifiers, typos, paraphrases, inflected-Russian forms, English/Spanish, mixed-language, code/filenames` — but **not** the cross-language category (\"query in one language matching a conversation in another\"), which the source research (§21) and the cross-report both explicitly call for. If it's not in the phase-1 dataset now (recorded, unasserted — like paraphrases), you can't prove the phase-3 cross-lingual lift later, for the one scenario you personally care most about.\n\n**3. The interim answer is the model, and it's currently nondeterministic.** \"Ask model to find it in english\" goes through `search_conversations` (`query`, `limit`) in a bounded tool loop. The model *can* fan out — query in EN, then RU, then ES — and bridge languages today with zero infrastructure. But nothing in the tool description or spec tells it to, so whether it retries in Russian depends on model mood and whether it knows you're multilingual. A one-line tool-description change (\"user's history spans Russian/Spanish/English; retry the query in those languages before concluding no match\") is the cheapest cross-lingual recall you will ever ship, and it remains useful *after* embeddings land (translation + lexical exactness sometimes beats vectors).\n\nOne more lever, lower value: chat **titles**. If titles are generated in the conversation's language (likely), they're cross-lingually unsearchable too — and title is often what the palette user scans. I wouldn't force English titles (bad UX), but it's a reason the embedding leg, not lexical tricks, is the right architectural bet.\n\n## Prioritized\n\n1. **Add the cross-language category to the phase-1 eval dataset now** (recorded, unasserted). Tiny diff to an existing artifact; without it, #197's acceptance for your scenario is unmeasurable. Fix the spec sentence while at it.\n2. **Amend the `search_conversations` tool description** to instruct multilingual query fan-out. Works today, ~zero cost, directly your \"ask the model in English\" flow.\n3. **Leave #196/#197 sequencing alone** — deferred backlog is correct; the MCP/knowledge milestone is higher leverage than better search, and the operator-config/Ollama embedding gate (BYOK resolution) is already settled in the cross-report. Just know that until then, the honest answer to \"will llame find my Russian discussion from an English query\" is *only if the model translates the query for you*.\n\nWant me to capture 1–2 as spec deltas / an issue, or keep exploring (e.g. how phase 4 `#198` recall-dispatch changes the multilingual story)?",
