gory must exist first.\n\nThe note also catalogs what *not* to do (index-time document translation, transliteration in `normalizeForSearch`, English-pinned titles, embedding debiasing) with reasons, and ends with the four candidate deltas (§6) held un-applied: eval category + spec sentence, #197 per-category reporting, the `search_conversations` multilingual-retry sentence, qwen3 in the #196 bake-off.\n\nSources used: [The Cross-Lingual Cost](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.07543), [What Drives Cross-lingual Ranking?](https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.19324), [query-embedding interpolation](https://arxiv.org/html/2606.13537v1), [LangSAE](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2601.04768), [LAReQA](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2004.05484), [CLIRudit](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2504.16264), [M3-Embedding](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2402.03216), [BentoML embedding guide](https://www.bentoml.com/blog/a-guide-to-open-source-embedding-models), [Ollama embedding roundup](https://www.morphllm.com/ollama-embedding-models), [Milvus comparison](https://milvus.io/blog/choose-embedding-model-rag-2026.md).\n\nOpen thread if you want to keep going: whether #198's recall-dispatch framing should tell the model *when* to fan out languages (e.g. only after a zero/weak first result) versus always — that's a token-budget vs. recall tradeoff worth a small experiment once the tool-loop budget semantics land.",
